\par
\tab e) Hank Maurer.  Hank worked on many different string-\par
\tab    manipulation languages (some of which actually completely\par
\tab    function) such as POLY SNOBOL.  He is also the original author\par
 \tab    of DEC's DIBOL compiler as first released in the hacked-\par
\tab    together system known as DIBOL-8 which was later translated\par
 \tab    into a newer system known as COS-300/310.\page When the TC01 and TU55 drive were installed in the Brooklyn Poly 4K PDP-8 system (in an adjacent full-size cabinet), these five people spent about four months designing and implementing a complete operating system using only their brains and hand-toggling in all the binary instructions on the front panel of the computer.  (Note: This process is not only tedious, but has no margin for error.  This includes hand assembly of the program, often just a bunch of hand-written notes on napkins from the local Chinese restaurant the members of this group frequented in the nearby New York City Chinatown area, a relatively short subway ride from downtown Brooklyn where the school is located.)\par
\par
When just about all of the basic software was toggled into memory in various sections and written to designated tape blocks, the first bootable system was created.  This became known as the R-L Monitor System (after the initials of Richard Lary and Lennie Elekman who performed most of the work along with the design notions and inspiration of the other three].  (Note: Virtually all important PDP-8 software of merit was written by this group and the follow-on or "second generation" group.  The five all went to work for DEC at the same time, and established themselves as the William Claude Fields Memorial Programming Group (WCFMPG) sometimes known as the Friends of the Boston Massacre.\line\line This "second generation" is generally known as the Poly ? Society\par
(P?S).  Members of the P?S are known for various programming (and related) works; some of them eventually became DEC employees to work along side some of the members of the WCFMPG.  What DEC never understood is that this dual-based homogeneous group was actually at least partly responsible for the design of all the work they perceived they were managing; interference with the strong bonds of this collection of friends proved detrimental to PDP-8 programming in the long run.  Later in this document, some examples of this problematic management style will be detailed.\par
\par
While all of the members of the WCFMPG were present and participated in the creation of the R-L Monitor System, they also became users of this admittedly imperfect, but quite viable, operating system.  (Note: We can mostly overlook most of the imperfections given the extraordinary nature of how the software was created; however, it was never actually finished in any sense of the word because the entire WCFMPG left to work for DEC.  Thus, any further development would be accomplished primarily by members of the P?S.)\par
\par
The R-L Monitor System is extremely pragmatic, designed to take advantage of every aspect of the limited PDP-8 configuration it was created on.  It is only capable of being run on a single DECtape drive (either a TU55 or a TU56 half-pair of that newer drive) connected to either a TC01 or the later TC08 which is fully compatible and also can be configured to connect to any model up to and including the PDP-8/A.  Without bus conversion, the TC01 can only be interfaced to the original PDP-8 and the PDP-8/I.  However, these are merely hardware configuration considerations; from a software standpoint they are identical.\page Within the R-L Monitor System are two different file systems, one solely for the core-image of system programs that can have a collection of input files passed, and separately a file system of fixed-length files (later known as the Tiny File System or TFS).  The separation minimizes the overhead associated with the execution of a program while the separate directory of TFS file further improves the overall command throughput.\par
\par
The TFS files are always exactly 2048 (2K) words long situated at fixed intervals on the DECtape.  A file may be modified with the intention of in-place replacement of the former file.  As such, it is generally unnecessary to access the TFS directory (unless an actual update occurs, which is very rare during much of the program development cycle).\line\line The TFS file structure is very straight-forward, lacking frills that some other systems add much later in the development of software in general (and PDP-8 software in particular); it has the advantage of being completely present in memory whenever the keyboard monitor is actively processing commands.  Thus, the keyboard monitor is actually a hybrid of an execution initiator, a file maintenance tool and a file contents text editor with a completely cached file directory.\par
\par
TFS files can contain any combination of text characters desired; the implementation is neutral with regard to programming language used.  Ordinary text can be entered for the purpose of providing documentation notes (albeit this is not a "word processing" system in the modern sense).  Note: More primitive systems such as the LINC LAP operating systems can only contain valid assembly language input; the\par
files have to be "compromised" to avoid syntactic errors relative to the prevailing assembly language rules.\par
\par
TFS text files contain internal permanent line numbers cleverly arranged at the end of the file; this allows reading the file from the beginning while ignoring the line numbers.  As such, any programming language can be accommodated.  (Note: At first glance, this superficially resembles the nature of BASIC-only systems; the difference it that in BASIC the line numbers must be strictly retained or elaborate means of resequencing the line numbers in the context of the BASIC language must be available.  This capability is a feature of POLY BASIC since that operating system is strictly for BASIC programs.  TFS text format is essentially free-form text with line numbers to determine position order within the file.)\line\line As needed, the line numbers can be instantly resequenced within a copy of the program loaded into the dedicated edit buffer of the keyboard monitor; no permanent changes are made until the buffer is written to a file on the DECtape.  Resequencing line numbers is sometimes needed to allow newer text lines to be interspersed with existing lines which may have run out of free incremental line numbers in the pertinent section of the file.  (Note: Line numbers generally start at 100 and increment by ten for each line.  Should there not be sufficient lines between any two of the original lines, the resequence allows a fresh increment to allow additional new text lines, etc.)\page Many are unfamiliar with this form of "direct access" file editing.  The usual method of file editing requires at least an equal amount of additional storage as the entirety of the program currently occupies.  Then the entire program is read into a buffer in small sections, and the entire program is eventually written out to another file.  As a practical matter, additional space is needed as during development, the size of the file being developed increases.\line\line In direct access editing, each portion of a file has its own unique name and the ability to be individually edited without any interaction with the rest of the file stream.\line\par
There is an occasional problem with this regimen: If the capacity of the latest file being edited is exceeded, it will be impossible to add in some portion of the current editing operations.  The programmer must remedy this situation by splitting the file into two sections; commands are available to define the dividing line number for the splitting operation.  An available file name must be available (which could have been already placed into the TFS directory to avoid overhead) so that each "half" of the problematic file is separately stored in its own file.  This is quickly accomplished; the programmer must understand the new logical stream of files that now applies.  The programmer can then go back and finish the latest editing session.\line\line While this situation is admittedly distracting, in practice it happens quite infrequently; proper preparation for future editing operations can mitigate this.  (Note: Future P?S/8 features were added to slightly ease effective recovery with this situation after several repetitions.)  When compared to the overhead of entire file editing (especially on DECtape) a programmer proficient in the relevant techniques can produce meaningful results in far less time (and the scope of projects can be much larger because there is no need to allocate additional space).  A typical edit may affect only one percent of the aggregate source file size, yet in conventional designs, the entire file contents has to be read and written each time any form of edit is needed.\par
\par
While limited in various ways, the R-L Monitor System was used by every member of the WCFMPG or P?S while physically present at Brooklyn Poly.  No other system could be so effectively used on the available hardware.  Competing designs (even in the future) were frustratingly slow and discouraging.\par
\par
Unless the scope and size of a project is far longer than reasonable to store on a single DECtape drive, the R-L Monitor was extremely effective as the operating system of choice among the small collections of users exposed to it.  (Note: Many members of the P?S became computer consultants in the greater New York area; as such, "pockets" of users of the R-L Monitor System appeared over the next few years.)\par
